  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nThursday and Friday are still shaping up to be wet with heavy \nrainfall and the potential for flash flooding. High rain chances \nremain in the forecast through the weekend with a cold front \npossibly stalling along the Texas coast by Friday night into \nSaturday. There remains a lot of uncertainty with regard to the cold \nfront's timing, strength, and where it will ultimately stall across \nS TX. \n\nA long wave mid/upper level trough with a cut-off low centered over \nthe Desert SW, will continue to slowly move east-southeast through \nthe period. Deeper moisture is already advecting across S TX from \nthe gulf in the low and mid levels with PWATs already around 2.2 \ninches. The low will also tap into mid/upper level Pacific moisture \nfrom the remnants of Polo with PWATs progged to increase to 2.4-2.5 \ninches with some models indicating 2.7 inches moving across S TX on \nThursday. This is well above normal for this time of year. \n\nThis deep moisture combined with a developing surface trough over S \nTX, and embedded upper level short waves tracking northeast ahead of \nthe main low, will lead to a high chance of showers and \nthunderstorms across the region Thursday. The highest chances up to \n90% are forecast for the Brush Country and Rio Grande Plains to 40% \nalong the coastline. There is a Marginal Risk of severe \nthunderstorms on Thursday. Some storms may become strong to severe \nwith wind damage expected to be the main threat.\n\nThe high chance of widespread convection will continue through \nFriday as the upper low draws nearer to S TX and brings a cold front \nto the area. The cold front is forecast to move into the Rio Grande \nPlains and Brush Country on Friday and possibly stall somewhere \nalong the coast line Friday night into Saturday morning. There \nremains uncertainty as to the timing, strength and just how far \nsoutheast the cold front tracks. Where ever the front stalls will be \nthe main focus for the heaviest rainfall and highest risk for flash \nflooding. For now, it looks like it will stall Friday night along \nthe coast, then push off the coast by Sunday. A second re-enforcing \ncold front is possible by Monday with winds strengthening from the \nnortheast and maintaining the cooler temperatures across S TX. The \nupper low is forecast to finally exit to the east with drier \nconditions expected next week. \n\nTemperatures are tricky for Thursday. With the increasing \nclouds/rain, the max temps are expected to be slightly cooler than \ntoday. However, if less cloud cover develops, this will allow higher \ntemps across the area which would combine with the high humidity and \nlead to heat indices up to 110-112 across the eastern CWA.  Will \nmonitor trends for Heat Advisory conditions Thursday. Cooler \ntemperatures are expected Friday and into next week.\n\nMinor coastal flooding is a possibility at times during high tide \nthrough early next week, especially Monday due to strengthening \nnortheast winds across the gulf waters behind the second cold front.",
